  "summary": "Recent preprints dissect activity-dependent synaptic and firing changes in hippocampal circuits that underlie memory recall and plasticity.",
  "synthesis": "These preprints converge on activity-dependent control of hippocampal synaptic strength and firing as a substrate of memory. In CA1, glutamatergic input from the lateral entorhinal cortex rises during object and Barnes maze recall, silencing this pathway impairs recall, and optogenetic input induces LTP. Persistent dendritic spine shrinkage during mGluR-induced LTD depends on coincident neuronal activity. At hippocampal mossy fiber synapses, GABAB-receptor presynaptic inhibition shifts transmission from detonation to subdetonation mode and limits vesicle pool plasticity. Blocking translation in dorsal CA1 pyramidal cells impairs novelty-induced increases in population firing while preserving sharp-wave ripple reactivation of newly formed assemblies. The distinctive contribution is the dissociation of firing-rate gain from assembly formation, together with the requirement for neuronal activity in gating structural LTD.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
